# Line endings, declared once so tooling stops deciding per-machine.
#
# ── WHY THIS EXISTS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` was set locally, but the blobs in this repository were
# committed with CRLF — so the two disagreed. The result was that any edit made
# by a tool writing LF (a script, a codegen pass, an agent, an editor on a
# non-Windows machine) silently rewrote EVERY line of the file, and the change
# arrived as a whole-file diff with the real edit buried inside it. That is not
# a cosmetic problem: it makes review and `git blame` useless on the one commit
# where you most need them.
#
# The fix is to make the repository the authority instead of each checkout:
# LF in the object store, CRLF in the working tree on Windows. A one-time
# renormalisation commit brought the existing blobs in line; it is listed in
# `.git-blame-ignore-revs` so it does not pollute blame.

* text=auto eol=crlf

# ── EXEMPTIONS: FILES LINUX EXECUTES ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are consumed by a shell or a container at runtime, where a trailing CR
# is a syntax error rather than whitespace — `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` fails with
# "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M". They keep LF in the working tree too, so a
# checkout that is rsync'd or built into an image stays runnable.
*.sh            text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint* text eol=lf

# ── BINARY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Declared explicitly so no EOL conversion is ever attempted on them.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
